a {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] ["question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta ){\\cancel{cos}}^{2}(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\cancel{sin}\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 0-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/32/32a586a1fe160a6c3a2cfee3cfb78af3d596679cf40abedeb0b8d622a3cf736c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] ["question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b"] ["answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/id #uuid "fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958"] ["answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/id #uuid "e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591"] ["answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/id #uuid "c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39"] ["answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/id #uuid "d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda"] ["answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/id #uuid "742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392"] ["answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/value "None of the statements are true"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/correct "answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/choices "answer-fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/choices "answer-e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/choices "answer-c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/choices "answer-d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda"] ["answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/choices "answer-742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392"] ["question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b"] [17592186188297 :knowledge-point/questions "question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d"] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/id #uuid "3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1"] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-144911"] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(5{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b"] ["answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/id #uuid "0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310"] ["answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/id #uuid "4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3"] ["answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/id #uuid "a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6"] ["answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/id #uuid "7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad"] ["answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/id #uuid "e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9"] ["answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/value "None of the statements are true"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/correct "answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/choices "answer-0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/choices "answer-4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/choices "answer-a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/choices "answer-7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad"] ["answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/choices "answer-e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9"] ["question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b"] [17592186188297 :knowledge-point/questions "question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1"] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/id #uuid "1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0"] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145039"] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/id #uuid "fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656"] ["answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/id #uuid "c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da"] ["answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/id #uuid "f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e"] ["answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/id #uuid "846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914"] ["answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/id #uuid "864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a"] ["answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/id #uuid "70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8"] ["answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/correct "answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/choices "answer-c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/choices "answer-f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/choices "answer-846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/choices "answer-864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a"] ["answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/choices "answer-70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8"] ["question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656"] [17592186188316 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0"] [17592186188613 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [17592186188613 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+{0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\theta (2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\cancel{\\theta }(2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the left limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/56/564cb7c3d166e60b474a6e980c4932b7eb97695efb212368c437aeea4c59b8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186188613 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] [17592186185958 :answer-field/correct 17592186185960] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/id #uuid "3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25"] ["step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/id #uuid "48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6"] ["step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/id #uuid "5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b"] ["step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/id #uuid "63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b"] ["step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/id #uuid "4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832"] ["step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0" :step/id #uuid "4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0"] ["step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/content "question-fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90"] ["step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/next "step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b"] ["step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/content "question-a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d"] ["step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/next "step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b"] ["step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/content "question-3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1"] ["step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/next "step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832"] ["step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/content "question-1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0"] ["step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/next "step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0"] ["step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0" :step/content 17592186188613] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/title "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/type :activity.type/review] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/scope 17592186049521] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/course 17592186046673] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/steps "step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/steps "step-5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/steps "step-63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/steps "step-4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/steps "step-4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0"] ["activity-3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/first-step "step-48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6"]] "retractions" [[17592186188613 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → 0^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."]]}]